  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *sixths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/ec09e83087ba7ada6af5cf1d2d19e4260a4581f2818659ebedc902f8edf13250.png)\n\n \n\n- The sixths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{4}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/83ab830b24af9dddc5bb84d4496e440e4bb9d033b07df506001073a4b994a181.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $3$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/e7f2afcd0b6d934b639d96300d9fc149d15beebdef4dc4b3541677f6eb9d6de2.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $2$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{4}{6},$ we get $3$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n2÷\\boxed{\\frac{4}{6}}=3\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\frac{4}{6}.$",
